  "before_interfaces": {
    "lem-qft-free-time-ordered-kernel": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Fix the scalar shell parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ and all shell, Fourier, and metric conventions of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and $\\Box=-\\partial_t^2+\\Delta_{\\mathbf x}$. For $\\phi\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define the partial spatial Fourier transform\n$$\\widehat\\phi_t(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\phi(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x$$\nand define $H_\\mu\\in\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^4)$ by\n$$\\langle H_\\mu,\\phi\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R}dt\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)}e^{-i\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)|t|}\\widehat\\phi_t(-\\mathbf k).$$\nThis is a continuous distributional extension of the free scalar time-ordered two-point function: on $t>0$ its kernel is the Wightman two-point distribution $W_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)$, and on $t<0$ it is $W_\\mu(-t,-\\mathbf x)$. The displayed partial Fourier formula fixes the extension at $t=0$; no product of a step function with an unspecified singular distribution is used. The kernel is symmetric under $(t,\\mathbf x)\\mapsto(-t,-\\mathbf x)$, agrees with the two orderings on spacelike-separated supports, and obeys\n$$ (\\Box-\\mu^2)H_\\mu=i\\delta_0 $$\nin $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^4)$. There is no extra prefactor $i$ in the definition of $H_\\mu$."
    ],
    "lem-qft-free-action-equations": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nFix $\\mu\\ge0$, $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\\Box=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b$, and the Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]]; $\\mu$ is the inverse-length mathematical shell parameter of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. For a smooth real scalar field $\\phi$ and smooth commuting complex spinor $\\psi:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$, use the normalized free Lagrangian densities\n$$\\mathcal L_\\phi=-\\frac12\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi\\,\\partial_b\\phi+\\mu^2\\phi^2\\right),\\qquad \\mathcal L_D=\\bar\\psi\\left(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu\\right)\\psi,\\qquad \\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta.$$\nFor compactly supported smooth real variations $h$ and compactly supported smooth complex spinor variations $\\chi$, the action symbols are used only through the following finite, compactly supported first variations; no global integrability of the background fields is assumed:\n$$\\delta S_\\phi(\\phi;h)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}h(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi\\,d^4x,\\qquad \\delta S_D(\\psi;\\chi)=2\\operatorname{Re}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\chi^\\dagger\\beta\\left(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu\\right)\\psi\\,d^4x.$$\nEach field is stationary under all such variations if and only if its corresponding Euler–Lagrange equation holds pointwise:\n$$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi=0,\\qquad (i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0.$$\nThe adjoint Dirac equation follows from the second equation because the fixed matrices satisfy $(\\gamma^a)^\\dagger\\beta=\\beta\\gamma^a$. These are mathematical variation identities for commuting classical fields; they assert no quantization, Grassmann calculus, or physical measurement."
    ],
    "lem-qft-free-domain-observables": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice and use the scalar and Dirac free-field constructions of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] and [[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]] with their current draft hypotheses, including the Dirac projection and CAR inputs in [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. For a real scalar test $f\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$, $\\overline{\\Phi_\\mu(f)}=\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)$ is self-adjoint on the exact domain given by [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]]. On scalar bosonic Fock space, $N_s=d\\Gamma(1)$, $H_s=d\\Gamma(\\omega_\\mu)$, and $P_{s,j}=d\\Gamma(-k_j)$ are the maximal real multipliers of [[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]]; in particular,\n$$D(N_s)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\},$$\nand the Hamiltonian and momentum domains are exactly the square-integrability domains of the sums of their one-particle multipliers.\n\nOn the specified Dirac Fock space, spacetime-smeared fields $\\Psi(F)$ are bounded, while the translation generators $H_D,P_{D,j}$ have the exact maximal domains and joint PVM stated in [[lem-qft-dirac-fermionic-translation-spectrum]]. For any one of these self-adjoint real-multiplier operators $A$, a normalized $\\Psi\\in D(A)$ has finite mean $\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle$ and finite variance\n$$\\operatorname{Var}_\\Psi(A)=\\|(A-\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle)\\Psi\\|^2.$$\nMembership in $D(A)$ suffices for finite variance and does not require $\\Psi\\in D(A^2)$. A product $AB\\Psi$ is defined only when $\\Psi\\in D(B)$ and $B\\Psi\\in D(A)$, as specified in [[lem-nrqm-uncertainty-schrodinger-robertson-anticommutator-bound]].\n\nFor the scalar field, smooth spacetime tests approximating a point mass produce vacuum vectors with norms diverging to infinity. For the equal-time Dirac CAR field, spatial tests approximating a point mass produce bounded smeared operators whose operator norms diverge. Thus neither construction gives a point-field value in the indicated norm topology. The supplied free-field results define only smeared fields and do not define coincident diagonal products."
    ],
    "ex-qftmath-timeordered-jump": [
      null
    ]
  }
